WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.000
Every game lives on The Logic Grid's game cards. Tap NINETIMESNINE to open it.

2
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:20.700
Choose a difficulty, from Basic for a gentle start to Master, the hardest patterns. Each level names the logic it uses.

3
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:29.300
Pick 6×6 or 9×9, add Extra rules like Diagonals or Odd and even, or tap Today's puzzle: one fresh puzzle for everyone, every day.

4
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:37.400
More options: a colour theme, silent mode, sound effects, puzzles tuned to how you play, notes that clear themselves, and highlighting.

5
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:45.400
We'll play an Easy 9×9 in Classic, the traditional game. Tap Start puzzle: it's built just for you, with exactly one answer.

6
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:55.500
Each puzzle has a title and a short story. The timer and your progress sit on the right.

7
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.200
Fill every empty cell with a number from 1 to 9.

8
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.000
Each row holds 1 to 9 exactly once…

9
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.800
…each column holds 1 to 9 exactly once…

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.900
…and so does each of the nine 3×3 boxes.

11
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:20.500
The numbers already on the grid are your clues. They can't be changed, and they're enough to reach the one answer by logic alone.

12
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:31.100
Row 5, column 3 can only be 1: every other number is already in its row, column or box.

13
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:38.800
Row 4, column 1 can only be 4: every other number is already in its row, column or box.

14
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:46.500
Row 4, column 3 can only be 8: every other number is already in its row, column or box.

15
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:54.200
Row 4, column 2 can only be 3: every other number is already in its row, column or box.

16
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:02.000
That's the whole game: find a cell with only one possible number, fill it in, and look again. No guessing, ever.

17
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:14.500
Not sure yet? Switch on Notes and tap numbers to pencil in what a cell could be. Shift with a number does the same on a keyboard.

18
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:22.200
Fill notes writes in every number each empty cell could still be, so you can cross them off as you go.

19
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.500
Undo and Redo take back any move. Erase clears the cell you're on.

20
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.900
Stuck? Hint starts with a gentle nudge, not the answer.

21
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.400
Ask again to see where to look.

22
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:43.100
Ask once more and it explains why, in plain words. Then fill it in yourself, or let the hint do it.

23
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.300
Let's make a mistake on purpose. Check finds wrong numbers and marks them in red.

24
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.800
Undo takes it back. Or switch on Show mistakes as I play to see mistakes straight away.

25
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.400
Rules and keys keeps the rules close by. Print the puzzle, save it to a file, or start a new one from here too.

26
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.000
Keep going the same way until every cell is filled.

27
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:27.300
Solved! Tell the game how it felt. Your answer, and how you played, tune the next puzzle to you.

28
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:33.800
Watch the solution replays the logic one step at a time, with the reason for each number.

29
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:41.600
Next puzzle is always new: you never see the same puzzle twice. Share my result sends your time to friends.

30
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:49.700
Back on the setup page, your puzzles save as you play, and Stats and badges keeps your times, streaks and rewards.

31
00:03:53.100 --> 00:04:06.800
You and a computer rival take turns on one grid. A right number claims the cell; a wrong one costs your turn. Notes are free. Claim the most cells to win.

32
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:22.800
Place each number in the same row, column or box as your last one to grow a chain. Longer chains score more, forced numbers score double, and a wrong number breaks the chain.

33
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:37.600
Water hides some of the clues. Every right number drains the cells around it and brings clues up. If logic runs dry the tide ebbs by itself, so you never guess.

34
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:53.000
The numbers become glyphs. Finish a row for a Lantern, a column for a Compass and a box for a Key, and travel the quest map one realm at a time.
